<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<windowsServices>
<!--
  Windows services to check (VCF Content Factory vCommunity).

  This list matters only if the account's Guest OS Service Monitoring
  Status is Enabled. Name
  the services by their service name (the short name shown in the service's
  properties, not the display name). For each Windows virtual machine the
  adapter asks the guest for the state of each listed service.

  A name may hold letters, digits, spaces and the characters _ . $ # @ ( ) + -
  The first character must be a letter, a digit or an underscore, and a name
  is at most 256 characters long. Any other name is skipped and reported,
  because the name is put inside a command that runs in every monitored guest.
  A name that contains the two characters $( is skipped as well.

  Format
    - Separate entries with commas. A line break does NOT separate entries.
    - An XML comment, like this one, is ignored. To switch an entry on, move
      it out of the comment, and keep the comma after it.
    - Do not write two hyphens in a row inside a comment.

  Do not edit this shipped file: every upgrade of the pack replaces it and
  your edits are lost (measured on VCF Operations 9.0.2 and 9.1.1, even without force
  content update). Back the files up before an upgrade. Make a copy under another name in the VCF Operations configuration-file store, and
  enter that name (no .xml) in the account's Windows Service Configuration
  File field. As shipped, every entry is commented out, so no service is
  checked.

  Examples, one per line:
    W32Time,
    Spooler,
    EventLog,
    WinRM,
    MSSQLSERVER,
-->
</windowsServices>
